cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  gec-conformance: A Preregistered Conformance Protocol for
  Governed-Execution Claims
message: >-
  If you use this protocol or its instruments, please cite it using
  the metadata below.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Billy
    family-names: Davis
    name-suffix: Jr.
    affiliation: Hudson Forge Technologies LLC
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0000-4807-5851'
repository-code: >-
  https://github.com/billyrdavis1985-bot/gec-conformance
abstract: >-
  A preregistered conformance protocol for evaluating systems that
  claim to govern execution by admitting or holding state transitions
  against declared invariants. An independently authored capability is
  executed through the substrate under a contract frozen and hashed in
  advance; benign perturbations and deliberate violations are injected;
  and the substrate's decision and its stated reason are compared
  against predictions locked before execution. The primary outcome is
  predicate-level agreement rather than decision-level, because a system
  can reach a correct decision by an incorrect route. False-positive
  rate on negative controls is reported unconditionally, because a gate
  that holds on everything scores perfectly on violations and is
  worthless. Ships with a rule-agnostic canonicalization engine
  validated against the RFC 8785 published vectors, a 33-case
  adversarial differential corpus, a clean-room receipt verifier, a
  first-divergence locator, and a harness validated against six mock
  substrates with known defects.
keywords:
  - conformance testing
  - preregistration
  - canonicalization
  - RFC 8785
  - governed execution
  - admission control
  - reproducibility
  - clean-room implementation
license: MIT
version: 1.0.0
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22103548
date-released: '2026-08-25'
